g  against  i wanted to point to the fact that most  black americans are not  fully african like what you're doing is  you you are creating an identity  in order to pacify the white identity  that says this is what you are  even though you know four factors that's  not the entirety of you well you you've  never been to africa  no  you gotta go  man it's your motherland what are you  thinking this whiteness created these  things black people are like this asian  people are like this latino people were  like this  and we all had to adhere to these  fictions that were created for us i'm  just trying to stay real this navigation  of identity is tied to the show's  exploration of reality and what feels  real are black women considered  brunettes  i just held the balls and put them in  the jug  in this episode darius explains  simulation theory while van discovers  that drake isn't really at drake's new  year's eve party that all the photos  that were being posted on instagram were  with cardboard cutouts drake is a star  whose stardom is reinforced by a  projected artifice stars are just a  projection of what's actually already  inside of a model  what is real and why does everything  feel so strange  are donald glover and his team  intentionally creating an  afro-surrealist work with atlanta miller  pointed out that a lim smith the artist  commissioned to create the poster for  the show's third season explicitly  identifies as an afro surrealist and  that late fashion designer virgil abloh  who has collaborated with glover also  was familiar with the movement so it  seems likely that he's at the very least  familiar with the idea but whether or  not it's a conscious intent for the show  is hard to say as stephanie robinson one  of the lead writers on atlanta told the  guardian we certainly don't approach  episodes and say hey guys let's make  sure this script is surreal we're a very  specific group of individuals who are  probably more drawn to the unusual the  strange 